  <Scene>
    <WorldInfo title="Why Fine Detail Melts First" info="&quot;X3D Math Lab • Static sampled geometry and authored lesson parameters; not a proof, live controller, or checkpoint.&quot;">
      <MetadataString containerField="metadata" name="mathlab.sample" reference="mathlab.x3d/1" value="&quot;{\&quot;adapter\&quot;:\&quot;mathlab.x3d/1\&quot;,\&quot;axes\&quot;:\&quot;History sheet: horizontal x∈[0,2π] shown as x−π; vertical height=2(u−1); depth=4t/3−2 for time 0–3. Mint selects the current time; cyan is the initial profile.\&quot;,\&quot;bounds\&quot;:{\&quot;center\&quot;:[0.0,1.1102230246251565e-16,0.0],\&quot;size\&quot;:[6.911503837897545,3.9000000000000004,4.5]},\&quot;configuration_sha256\&quot;:\&quot;cc845bb4ac0a03c4b366b6ce6f9513fa9377cb582215a4dfbcd288d5887912bd\&quot;,\&quot;insights\&quot;:[\&quot;The source is an exact two-mode solution of the periodic linear heat equation on length 2π. There is no time-stepping solver, boundary heat loss, or random noise.\&quot;,\&quot;The sheet has one spatial coordinate and one time coordinate. Its displayed height is 2(u−1); depth maps time 0…3 to −2…2. This is not a two-dimensional spatial temperature field.\&quot;,\&quot;The spatial mean is fixed at one because every nonconstant cosine averages to zero over the periodic domain. Mean squared deviation is ½[0.45²e^(−2αt)+Aₘ²e^(−2αm²t)] and decreases.\&quot;,\&quot;Higher spatial frequency creates larger curvature at equal amplitude. The −m² eigenvalue of the second derivative explains faster smoothing without implying that heat travels as a wave.\&quot;,\&quot;The finite-difference residual contains spatial truncation and roundoff error. The separately reported periodic integrals check the mean and fluctuation formula against the sampled profile.\&quot;],\&quot;lesson\&quot;:\&quot;heat\&quot;,\&quot;metrics\&quot;:[{\&quot;id\&quot;:\&quot;mean\&quot;,\&quot;label\&quot;:\&quot;Spatial mean\&quot;,\&quot;note\&quot;:\&quot;Periodic trapezoid; analytic reference is exactly 1.\&quot;,\&quot;unit\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;value\&quot;:1.0},{\&quot;id\&quot;:\&quot;energy\&quot;,\&quot;label\&quot;:\&quot;Mean squared fluctuation\&quot;,\&quot;note\&quot;:\&quot;Spatial average of (u−1)², not total heat or thermodynamic energy.\&quot;,\&quot;unit\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;value\&quot;:0.08113829220026819},{\&quot;id\&quot;:\&quot;fine_amplitude\&quot;,\&quot;label\&quot;:\&quot;Remaining fine-scale amplitude\&quot;,\&quot;note\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;unit\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;value\&quot;:0.02402186715812318},{\&quot;id\&quot;:\&quot;rate_ratio\&quot;,\&quot;label\&quot;:\&quot;Fine / broad decay rate\&quot;,\&quot;note\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;unit\&quot;:\&quot;×\&quot;,\&quot;value\&quot;:25},{\&quot;id\&quot;:\&quot;pde_residual\&quot;,\&quot;label\&quot;:\&quot;Finite-difference PDE residual\&quot;,\&quot;note\&quot;:\&quot;|analytic uₜ−α·central-difference uₓₓ| at x=.73, h=.001. A stencil diagnostic, not a global error bound.\&quot;,\&quot;unit\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;value\&quot;:2.670600490195518e-07}],\&quot;parameters\&quot;:{\&quot;amplitude\&quot;:0.4,\&quot;diffusivity\&quot;:0.25,\&quot;mode\&quot;:5,\&quot;time\&quot;:0.45},\&quot;sample_schema\&quot;:\&quot;mathlab.sample/1\&quot;,\&quot;sample_sha256\&quot;:\&quot;0858b108e18cfd3c8bc824ad2f8c75015fe0975906350fad6adf12d7d092ec93\&quot;,\&quot;scope\&quot;:\&quot;Static sampled geometry and authored lesson parameters; not a proof, live controller, or checkpoint.\&quot;,\&quot;title\&quot;:\&quot;Why Fine Detail Melts First\&quot;,\&quot;view\&quot;:\&quot;spatial\&quot;}&quot;" />
    </WorldInfo>
